<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#roman-dutch-law">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman-Dutch law</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> law is the legal system that developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in the sixteenth to eighteenth centuries by combining Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> with Dutch custom, and was described by jurists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>. Carried to the Cape by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, it was kept by the British and remains the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and several neighbouring countries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Roman-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> law, in Dutch Rooms-Hollands recht, is uncodified: it rests on the writings of scholars and the decisions of judges rather than on a single code. It is a variety of the European continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, the ius commune. Though predominantly Roman in substance, it kept features drawn from Dutch feudal customary law, especially that of the province of Holland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> was largely abandoned in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, surviving in hybrid codes such as the Breviary of Alaric and in canon law. Interest revived after a copy of the Digest of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> reached northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> around 1070 and scholars at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> began to teach from it. Courts first applied the new learning where no local statute or custom applied, and later because judges found its concepts better suited to complex cases. This reception came slowly to northern Europe and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in the 15th century.</p><p>Italian lawyers led the first phase of this jurisprudence and French humanist scholars the second, in the 16th century. In the 17th and 18th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> had the most influence, through the jurists of the Hollandse Elegante School, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>, Johannes Voet, Ulrich Huber and Gerard Noodt, who merged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> with legal concepts of Dutch custom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> applied the system in their colonies, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, the Dutch West Indies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> and Dutch Ceylon. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> it ended abruptly in 1809, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s client state, adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>. The English, however, respected the existing law in the Dutch colonies they took, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>, Ceylon and the Cape, where it survived mixed with English law, a hybrid also called Anglo-Dutch law.</p><p>Colonies that stayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> did not keep it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> adopted a civil code in 1869 identical to the Old Dutch Civil Code of 1838. Roman-Dutch principles are still applied by the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and its neighbours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> they remain influential in land law despite an ordinance of 1917 that favoured English common law. The system also heavily influenced Scots law and introduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> the office of prosecutor, the schout-fiscaal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman-Dutch law” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lord Charles Somerset, governor from 1814 to 1826, controlled appointments, the press and land grants, and his autocracy led to the creation of an advisory council in 1825 and a charter of justice that introduced British-style courts while keeping <b>Roman-Dutch law</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Roman-Dutch law</b>, introduced in this period, still forms the basis of Sri Lankan civil law.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-ceylon">Dutch Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
